{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nu={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Read the retained receipt of experiment 7c1d823684194607b0fb103fc66bf7d3 (CheckRun 21d5c70cd8a9439594340e00f2d09c11, exit 0, 35 events). Its event 34 records three rounds of the same non-member sender's TimeoutNow trigger: leadership changed in all three rounds, the term advanced 3 to 4 to 5, and a leader was present after every round. The registered gap about repetition is answered by that measurement, the account gains the recurrence point, and a behavior entry records it.\\\",\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[\n  {\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@k-timeoutnow-repeat\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A single removed node can repeat the transfer trigger: three consecutive TimeoutNow requests from that node to surviving followers each displaced the leader, with the term advancing one per round (3, 4, 5) and a leader present after every round, so the disruption is repeatable rather than a one-off and the cluster keeps electing between attempts.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"A three-voter configuration from which one server has been removed while it stays transport-reachable; the requests are sent from the removed node's own transport to surviving followers that are not the current leader; shortened election timers.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-timeoutnow\\\",\\\"s-requestvote\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"21d5c70cd8a9439594340e00f2d09c11\\\":[34]}}\n ],\n \\\"gaps\\\":[\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"f59ffda2c1554082ae054646e92c2508-question-0\\\",\n   \\\"question\\\":\\\"Can a removed or otherwise non-member server repeat the TimeoutNow trigger so that leadership churn persists over consecutive rounds, and does the cluster reach a stable leader between attempts under the same isolation used for the single-trigger measurement?\\\",\n   \\\"response\\\":\\\"Measured: with the same removed sender, three consecutive triggers each changed leadership (rounds_with_leadership_change 3 of 3), the target's term advanced 3, 4, 5, and a leader was present after every round (rounds_with_a_stable_leader 3). The churn is therefore repeatable and the cluster does settle between attempts; both declared predicates hold on the receipt 21d5c70cd8a9439594340e00f2d09c11 at parsed event index 34.\\\"}\n ],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"f59ffda2c1554082ae054646e92c2508-question-0\\\",\n   \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"7c1d823684194607b0fb103fc66bf7d3-experiment\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-timeoutnow\\\",\\\"s-requestvote\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"21d5c70cd8a9439594340e00f2d09c11\\\":[34],\\\"e1ee764584564a95b3e288381dfb75d9\\\":[17,18]},\n   \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Two receipts answer this question: the earlier run (e1ee764584564a95b3e288381dfb75d9 at indices 17 and 18) measured one displacement from a removed sender, and this run (21d5c70cd8a9439594340e00f2d09c11 at index 34) repeats the trigger three times from the same sender, so the single displacement and the repetition come from the same scenario shape in the same harness file.\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: the transfer trigger is not restricted to the current leader, and the same non-member can use it repeatedly. Evidence: the earlier receipt shows one displacement with the serving leader stepping down and the target becoming leader; the later receipt shows three consecutive rounds, each changing leadership, with the term advancing 3, 4, 5 and a leader present after every round. Mechanism from source: timeoutNow clears the known leader, sets the recipient's state to Candidate and stores candidateFromLeadershipTransfer without inspecting the request's RPCHeader, so any transport-reachable peer can trigger the exchange as often as it can reach a voter; the campaign then sets LeadershipTransfer on its vote requests, and requestVote skips its leader-availability rejection when that flag is set, so voters that know a healthy leader still grant. Consequence: a removed or otherwise non-member server that remains transport-reachable can keep displacing the leader, one term per attempt, while the cluster still elects a replacement between attempts, so the effect is persistent disruption of authority rather than a stall or a loss of committed data. The measurement also corrects the account's earlier limitation, which said recurrence had not been measured.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n   \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n    \\\"The cluster reaches a leader between attempts, so each round costs one election and one term bump rather than leaving the cluster without leadership.\\\",\n    \\\"Any cluster member can force term changes by campaigning; what makes this path distinct is that it bypasses the voters' leader-availability protection, and that the sender need not be in the configuration at all.\\\",\n    \\\"The requests were sent from the removed node's own in-memory transport in a cluster that keeps it reachable, which is the situation of a server taken out of the configuration but not shut down; a deployment that retires the server entirely would not reach it.\\\",\n    \\\"No write path is affected: entries, logs and commitment remain consistent, and the disruption is confined to authority and term churn.\\\"\n   ],\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\n    \\\"Measured on one three-voter in-memory cluster with shortened timers and three rounds; longer repetition and larger clusters are not covered.\\\",\n    \\\"The probe chose each round's target as a surviving follower that was not the current leader, so the measurement covers that selection strategy rather than every possible target choice.\\\",\n    \\\"An earlier version of the probe was invalid because the request was delivered back to the sender; that wiring was fixed before the queued run, and the earlier invalid result (no leadership change) is superseded and not used as evidence.\\\"\n   ]}\n ]\n}\njson.dump(u,open('update.json','w'),indent=2); print('written')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -2\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
